{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/87344-werewolf-ethics","id":87344,"title":"Werewolf Ethics","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-03-09T01:13:26-08:00","id":87344,"title":"Werewolf Ethics","content":"I was struck by an interesting thought while refreshing myself on the Ridden. They are currently the antagonists in the vampire game I'm running (I know odd, but they want to play around with Blood Tenebrous), and I've just started playing an Ithaeur in a PBP. Well, enough about why the thought happened... lol, onto the idea.<br /> <br /> So, if, as a werewolf, you knew a non-Uratha (Wolf-Blooded, or just any reason for them to be important to the Uratha), would it be ethical to make a deal with a spirit to inhabit the significant person in order to save their life? What would it take for that line to be crossed? Or is it even a line?<br /> <br /> Almost immediately I could see arguments for both sides, and pros/cons.<br /> <br /> Then of course it got more complicated. What if the werewolf in question was one of the Pure?<br /> Would this be as ethical as making a Fetish? (I've played a Theurge (yes, 3rd edition) that had a huge issue with making fetishes, only a side note, and I've also played a Theurge that was 'Ridden' as well. Creepiest character I've ever played! From these two past perspectives, debating this in my own mind becomes an endless mute point.)<br /> <br /> I've decided if I get the chance I'll explore this concept through role-play. I would also be very amused to see other players' thoughts on this. <br /> <br /> A few of my own thoughts about it:<br /> <br /> Against:<br /> 1) It places the spirit on the wrong side of the gauntlet, and into one of the flock.<br /> 2) It disrupts the order of things, which the Uratha are to keep.<br /> 3) It is fundamentally altering the individual, it would be saving them by making them an abomination.<br /> For:<br /> 1) With a trusted spirit, and the right type of spirit, the changes will be minimal, and save the individual's life, and even better it.<br /> 2) What if the spirit used is the pack totem? A Wolf-Blooded would be elevated, and the Totem would be far more potent in assisting the pack. Plus loyalty is already a thing.<br /> 3) Not all spirits are bad, and spirits being on the material side of things do happen. How is it any different than making a fetish? So long as they are kept under control through proper spirit deals.<br /> <br /> Of course, none of these are full-proof, and are just simple first thoughts on each side. But I am super curious to see arguments on both sides that I didn't think of.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1033-lathcon","name":"Lathcon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1033_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"49"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-09T07:43:23-07:00","id":87476,"title":"","content":"1) With a trusted spirit, and the right type of spirit, the changes will be minimal, and save the individual's life, and even better it.<br /> <br /> I'll refute this below:<br /> <br /> 2) What if the spirit used is the pack totem?<br /> <br /> Then you lose your totem's benefits, given a spirit can't be bound to fleshy thing and a pack at the same time. It's written in the Claim numen.<br /> <br /> \u200b3) Not all spirits are bad, and spirits being on the material side of things do happen. How is it any different than making a fetish?<br /> <br /> There aren't &quot;bad&quot; or &quot;good&quot; spirits. Spirits are completely amoral.<br /> <br /> No. There aren't actual benevolent spirits. Even spirits which you would assume are &quot;good&quot; because they represent &quot;good&quot; things can fall into imbalance rather quickly, in a mad binge for essence. Take say, a spirit of healing. It doesn't goes out of it's way to heal people out of some sense of ethics. It does because it's compelled, like a junkie or like a obsessive compulsive to an alien concept of &quot;healing&quot;. What will it do with a mortal in it's grasp after say, fixing an hemorrhage? Prolly start to fix the little things, a scar here, a wart there, and by the time the spirit is over, the immune system is killing the person with what amounts to an auto-immune &quot;decease&quot;; not that the spirit recognizes it as such. When the guy is dead, it figures the person is now perfect - and will continue to seek out persons to make &quot;better&quot; because it gave it so much tasty essence.<br /> <br /> Fetishes aren't living beings. What you allow the spirit to do is to rape a person's soul until the abomination left is nothing resembling the original subject.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-09T13:28:00-07:00","id":87695,"title":"","content":"My werewolf did something similar (he was an idealist, and eventually became unplayable). It's not ethical at all, and being claimed is never going to improve someone's life, as their soul becomes subsumed by the thing that claims it. I suppose an easy to appreciate analogy would be the Embrace of a vampire. They may have powers, but that doesn't make their lives better. Or lives at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-09T17:30:32-07:00","id":87818,"title":"","content":"The only reason I can see for a werewolf to legitimately allow it would have to be with a mixed sense of values. Bale Hounds would probably jump all over it though... which should tell you exactly why the other Tribes wouldn't go for it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> WoD-Dark Eras!! (Backed for Viking Age Werewolf)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1939-lyrics-of-war","name":"Lyrics Of War","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1939_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1769"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-09T20:11:11-07:00","id":87895,"title":"","content":"I personally never liked the idea that all spirits always make the world a worse place from a human prospective. It seems that if that were the case, annihilating the shadow realm would be the appropriate response, not tending and cultivating a balanced shadow ecosystem of compatible spirit presences. <br /> <br /> In the story of Pangea, we find that Father Wolf didn't just put up a wall except as a last resort when his ability to police things was going to be cut short by his death. He demanded that spirits not overstay their time in the material world, but didn't tell them to never show their faces on the wrong side of the border. It seems to me that there are positive things that humans and spirits can get by interacting with one another that would be beneficial for both sides, including some forms of claiming. <br /> <br /> It'd certainly be frowned upon by the orthodoxy, but that doesn't make it ethcially wrong. You just need to make sure to serve your proper role as mediator and ensure that neither side is getting fucked over in this deal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1764-cesario","name":"Cesario","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"86"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-09T21:07:41-07:00","id":87915,"title":"","content":"In the detailing of the claiming process, neither the spirit or the host are lost, they are merged into a new 'spirit.' With memories and traits of both. Also, if the similarities of the host and the spirit are not close enough to begin with, they become an Urged, not Ridden. I don't think the embrace is an appropriate analogy for what happens. In the embrace, it is the SAME individual, mind, memories, etc., just passing into a new state of existence. Being claimed is the merging of two separate entities into one physical/spiritual body. <br /> <br /> What is Father Wolf's stance on claiming however? Or Mother Luna's for that matter? <br /> <br /> Werewolves, Mages, Geists, some Vampires, and super rarely the Lost and the Created, have positive relations and deals with Spirits. So too do Second Sighters. I must agree with Cesario on the standpoint of the spirit world not being black and white, despite it's being a sepia version of reality.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1033-lathcon","name":"Lathcon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1033_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"49"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-10T03:02:24-07:00","id":88041,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I personally never liked the idea that all spirits always make the world a worse place from a human prospective. It seems that if that were the case, annihilating the shadow realm would be the appropriate response, not tending and cultivating a balanced shadow ecosystem of compatible spirit presences. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> According to tribal lore, the Red Wolf, Sagrim-Ur spent his every waking hour asking Father why the rain fell, why the mountain stood, why the wind blew\u2026 For every question, Father Wolf\u2019s answer was the same. \u201cThey do, and it is good. Nothing more need be known.\u201d But Sagrim was never satisfied with this answer. He asked because he wanted to change things and watch what would happen if he could undo some of the rigid equilibrium that it was Father Wolf\u2019s duty to maintain. He would never have advocated Father Wolf\u2019s death, but neither did he act to stop it when the fateful attack came. Even as Father Wolf lay dying and snarling in impotent rage, Red Wolf stared at him in hidden wonder, imagining how things might be if they were allowed to proceed unchecked. But it was Sagrim himself who was changed when Father Wolf caught him and held him down under one weak paw to speak to him. \u201cThings will not be as they ought,\u201d he told his son. \u201cNote well how they go, and remember what I told you was good.\u201d </div> </div> </div>If the shadow is in balance, the different broods of spirits will police itself like a food chain. Introduce a new predator species and that balance can go out of whack. Picture the shadow like a Darwinian hellish nightmare. Introduce Claimed or Urged, who develop their depredations in the material realm (every spirit is a predator, yes, even a bunny spirit will cannibalize other bunny spirits) and their actions -will- fuck the balance up.<br /> <br /> Spirits you can &quot;trust&quot; are the ones which benefit to keep the status quo, (or the ones vying to change it into something you'd prefer) and this means normally the Jagglings, which are at the top of the food chain. If things aren't going well for you and it's only a matter of time before you get consumed, some spirits may gamble going into the material realm: where there aren't nearly as many competing predators. Others might chew up on inadequate essence, becoming magath, gaining power at the expense of any sanity or inner balance that the spirit displayed.<br /> <br /> Even Uratha's totem spirit, from Pack, Lodge or Tribe need to get something out of the relationship (copious amounts of essence, usually). But if you're allying with a race of monsters sworn to keep the balance of the world, it would be pretty stupid to take a leak on that notion when they're bringing the bacon to you. Nothing is free.<br /> <br /> Most spirits know their place. A combination of discouragement from nearby Uratha patrolling both sides; and/or by the difficulty to cross the Gauntlet (without a specific numen the locus needed to cross the gauntlet has to be of a similar resonance to that of the spirit's; hence why you'll be hard pressed to see, say, a bear spirit of low rank -IE, which only resonates with bears- taking a joy-ride. This usually requires the bear to be Jaggling, and say, has taken say the additional aspects of Endurance).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-10T03:27:05-07:00","id":88046,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cesario</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/87344-werewolf-ethics?p=87895#post87895\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I personally never liked the idea that all spirits always make the world a worse place from a human prospective. It seems that if that were the case, annihilating the shadow realm would be the appropriate response, not tending and cultivating a balanced shadow ecosystem of compatible spirit presences.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, it wouldn't. The existence and work of the Azlu serves as a demonstration of what happens if you cut off the Shadow from the Flesh - a world devoid of spirit and soul.<br /> <br /> The Flesh can't live without the Shadow. The Flesh can't live *with* the Shadow. This is why an external force to keep the balance is needed.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-10T03:35:40-07:00","id":88047,"title":"","content":"We had werewolves use spirits and spirit abilities for their own purposes quite a bit in our games. Sometimes it went ok, sometimes it went out of control.<br /> <br /> Making deals with spirits is fun, story-wise, especially in the context of demonstrating the ways these dealings can go wrong.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-13T11:42:24-07:00","id":90360,"title":"","content":"I don't think the Pack Totem is so much a spirit that is trustworthy as it is a spirit that has been altered significantly by its bonding with several powerful half spirit creatures. On a metaphysical level it has changed itself so that the pack is a part of itself, and any aid it offers them it is actually offering itself.<br /> <br /> In general though, you can trust a spirit to behave about as much as you can trust its real world counterpart, which is to say not at all. You can't possibly trust a rabbit not to chew on your crops, or a tree not to grow its roots up under the sidewalk, or love to not get a pair of idiot teens from warring families to elope and kill themselves. Things usually behave as their nature dictates, and they don't give a rats ass what anything else thinks about it. Spirits are like that but with a lot more power.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}